                     Welcome to Sidekick 2.0
    ----------------------------------------------------------

    This README file contains important information about
    Sidekick 2.0.  For the latest information about Sidekick 2.0 
    and the manuals, read this file in its entirety.


TABLE OF CONTENTS
-----------------
1.  Important Information
2.  Corrections to the Manual
3.  Files on the Disks

1. Important Information
------------------------
 
------------------------

  - Usage of Extended Memory: Sidekick can now use extended
    memory for its data swapping.  Sidekick assumes that this 
    extended memory is managed by an extended memory manager that 
    is compatible with the XMS standard. You can verify if 
    Sidekick is using extended memory by looking under SK | 
    Services | About Sidekick.  To specify the amount of Extended 
    or Expanded memory, run SKCONFIG and choose Memory | EMS 
    memory to use.  For more information see Chapter 1 of Getting 
    Started.

  - INSTALLING: You MUST use the INSTALL program to install
    Sidekick 2.0. The files on the distribution disks are    
    archived and have to be properly assembled.

  - REINSTALLING SIDEKICK 2.0: Reinstalling Sidekick 2.0 into the
    same directory causes all desktop files (*.sks files) to be 
    deleted.  If you wish to save these files, you must rename 
    them (to something other than *.sks) before you reinstall 
    Sidekick.

  - QEMM.SYS users: Do not use the command line switch or
    parameter "Frame=A000". Sidekick 2.0 uses this area.

  - NETWORK Users: The User Dictionary (USER.DIC) cannot be shared 
    on a network. In SKCONFIG, under Network, User-dictionary 
    directory should be a private directory.

    For 3+Open and other Lan Manager based networks, use the 
    SKCONFIG program and configure Sidekick 2.0 for PC-Lan 
    network. For 3Com and 3+Share networks, use the 3Com network 
    selection.

    On a NOVELL network your CAPTURE command (used for 
    redirecting printer output to your network printer) must 
    contain "/NT" for no tabs AND "/TI=15" for time out=15 
    seconds, for example,

          capture  /NT /TI=15

    Other networks may require a similar statement.

  - CONVERTING Sidekick 1.x and Sidekick Plus files: When 
    converting Sidekick 1.x Dialer files, you must define the 
    format of the dialer in a text file with the extension .fmt. 
    The following naming convention is used for these .fmt files.

    SKCONVER will first look for a file called <fname>.fmt
    where <fname> is the name of your Dialer file. Using the
    Sidekick 1.x default, this would be "phone.fmt".

    If <fname>.fmt file is not found, then SKCONVER will look
    for a file named "default.fmt".  This file is located in your 
    Sidekick 2.0. default directory.

    If neither of these files exist, then the conversion
    will not continue.

    The .fmt file must be in the same directory that contains
    SKCONVER. For details on how to structure the .fmt file, read
    the "default.fmt" file that is in your Sidekick 2.0 
    directory.

    Sidekick Plus TimePlanner files that are converted will have
    to be edited if any entries contain an alarm that dials a
    VOICE/DATA number. Sidekick Plus alarms use the "INDEX"
    field to specify the number to dial. Sidekick 2.0 uses
    "Last Name, First Name" fields for VOICE calls and the 
    "Service" field for DATA calls.

    SKCONVER does not convert the Sidekick Plus glossary, 
    therefore, you may have to do this conversion manually.
    Sidekick 2.0 communication passwords are entered under 
    Phone|Script passwords in Communications.

  - CHANGING SORT ORDER:
    Copy the appropriate .SOR file to PARADOX.SOR

    Delete all .PX files

    Run SKCONVER, using the Rebuild option, on each database 
    file.

    Rerun Sidekick.  If Sidekick is resident in memory, it will 
    not understand the new sort order until it has been run 
    again.

  - PRINTING: If you print using a "cut sheet feeder", make sure 
    you have set the dip switches correctly. Check your printer 
    manual for "out of paper" situations.

    Since the default Printer Settings are generic, you may want 
    to custom tailor them for your specific printer and 
    preferences. The settings are found and may be changed under 
    File|Printer Settings in each application.

    The following printer specific Printer Settings are 
    recommended for the configurations below:

    Printer/mode/size                Top   Bottom   Left   Right
    =================                ===   ======   ====   =====
    HP DeskJet 500/graphics/A4      0.500  0.600   0.750   0.500
    QMS ColorScript/graphics/letter 1.200  0.700   0.750   0.500
    QMS PS-410a/graphics/A4         0.700  0.500   0.750   0.500

    When printing text to an HP LaserJet type printer and your 
    file contains extended ASCII characters, you will need to 
    send the appropriate initialization string to the printer. 
    Check your printer's documentation. 

  - ATTACHED NOTES: Maximum file size for attached note in Time 
    Planner is 6,000 characters. Maximum size for attached notes 
    in Address Book and Communications is 48,000 characters.

  - Installing under Windows 3.0:
    a.) Install Sidekick 2.0 as a stand-alone program.

    b.) Type "Win" from the DOS prompt.  If Sidekick 2.0 is        
    installed in a directory other than the default directory 
    (C:\sk2), you must change the path in the sk2.pif file. If 
    you used the default go to c.) below.

        Open the PIF editor from the Windows 3.0 Program 
        Manager. (Normally in the Accessories window.)

        Open the file "sk2.pif" found in the directory where 
        Sidekick 2.0 was installed.

        Change the path in both the Program Filename AND Start- 
        up Directory fields.  Leave all other fields the same.

        Save the sk2.pif file and close the PIF editor.

    c.) Move to the program group where you want the Sidekick        
    icon to be located.  From the Program Manager, select 
    File|New, then select Program Item, and select OK.

        In the Program Item Properties dialog, enter Sidekick 2.0 
        in the description.

        Select the Browse button, and change the filename mask to 
        *.pif.

        Move to the directory where Sidekick 2.0 is installed, 
        and select sk2.pif.

        Select OK to exit the dialog box and return to the 
        Program Manager.

    d.) Windows will put Sidekick 2.0 into the program group that 
    was highlighted when File|New was chosen. To move Sidekick 
    2.0 into a different program group, use the mouse to drag the 
    program icon into a new/different program group.


